{"authored" [{"question" "q-112960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a63942f66d56f39394a0366071950783d76f1609c9bf086f763aa736f1d9bd1" "basis" 510 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a63942f66d56f39394a0366071950783d76f1609c9bf086f763aa736f1d9bd1" "basis" 510 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7923c4355bf503b14c87a933ca68f9ea473ced09004460765e66edc0ac8ae7c2" "basis" 510 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a63942f66d56f39394a0366071950783d76f1609c9bf086f763aa736f1d9bd1" "basis" 510 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a63942f66d56f39394a0366071950783d76f1609c9bf086f763aa736f1d9bd1" "basis" 510 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/state.json" "file_