stion-capture/13947117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd2b3ff23cf9c6fe93518c3aa146eb14284c5ffdb837029ee7600f6f56371db4" "basis" 565 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }9}{\\text{factor of }-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd2b3ff23cf9c6fe93518c3aa146eb14284c5ffdb837029ee7600f6f56371db4" "basis" 565 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85f96c4009bf9e110702f2a3df1a6a9b99eca4c