on"} {"question" "q-112877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd2b3ff23cf9c6fe93518c3aa146eb14284c5ffdb837029ee7600f6f56371db4" "basis" 565 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-112877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd2b3ff23cf9c6fe93518c3aa146eb14284c5ffdb837029ee7600f6f56371db4" "basis" 565 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949258/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-38293" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6$ has one of its roots in the interval $(0,3),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-38293" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,3),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=1,-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n1-2+3=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-38293" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cb941bad97d9e40443f3af406a1d4779a6af4f85dd46fa18df81974e49fe4e2"} {"question" "q-38293" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-38293" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/