actor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cb941bad97d9e40443f3af406a1d4779a6af4f85dd46fa18df81974e49fe4e2"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-112960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cb941bad97d9e40443f3af406a1d4779a6af4f85dd46fa18df81974e49fe4e2"} {"question" "q-323218" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323218" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cb941bad97d9e40443f3af406a1d4779a6af4f85dd46fa18df81974e49fe4e2"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{7}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14049709/state.json" "file_sha256" "840488668e6508ff2d8d47ba535b9d065437be4da0305957022e4e173e53169d"} {"question" "q-323819" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/h